    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d295">

                <pb facs="0292" n="285" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg285"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9037a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">at
                        Peiping</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-28T15:00:00-04:00">July 28, 1937—3 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>141. Did a Japanese liaison officer inform Senior Commandant on or about July 27
                    that the Japanese intended to launch an attack on Chinese forces both within and
                    without Peiping?<anchor n="1" xml:id="d295-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d295-f1" n="1" xml:id="d295-f1-content">
                <p>The Embassy’s telegram No. 325, July 29, 2 p.m., replied: “The Senior Commandant
                    at no time received from a Japanese liaison officer information” to this effect.
                    “The nearest approach to that information is that contained in paragraphs 4 and
                    5 of my 302 of July 27, 1 p.m.” (793.94/9063)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
